</node>
<node id="NoSQL Injection">
  <data key="d0">NoSQL Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">A weakness class where malicious input is inserted into NoSQL queries that lack proper sanitization or syntax checking.&lt;SEP&gt;A weakness class where NoSQL database queries are constructed by concatenating user input with query or command structures, bypassing validation.&lt;SEP&gt;A weakness in MongoDB application logic where unsanitized or improperly parameterized input in the `$where` operator leads to execution of arbitrary JavaScript code within the database query context.&lt;SEP&gt;A weakness class where malicious input targeting custom NoSQL syntax in API calls, JSON structures, or query operators manipulates data processing, bypassing validation checks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NoSQL Database">
  <data key="d0">NoSQL Database</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of database that uses document-oriented or key-value arrangements rather than tabular schemas.&lt;SEP&gt;A non-SQL database that provides flexible data models, document-oriented storage, and varied query interfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;The broader category of database systems that store and return data in formats like JSON, XML, BSON, and other document or key-value models rather than relational tables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MongoDB">
  <data key="d0">MongoDB</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">The most widely used NoSQL database as of the author's reference point, frequently used in examples.&lt;SEP&gt;The most prevalent NoSQL database used for examples, featuring a document model and query API.&lt;SEP&gt;A NoSQL database platform that accepts MongoDB-specific query operators and allows JavaScript evaluation when certain input fields are not properly sanitized or parameterized.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific NoSQL document-oriented database commonly targeted by NoSQL injection tests and examples in the provided methodology.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JSON">
  <data key="d0">JSON</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A data format used in NoSQL APIs that can be manipulated to inject malicious code or alter query logic when validation checks fail.&lt;SEP&gt;A lightweight data interchange format used to transmit structured data in the request body.&lt;SEP&gt;The data format whose deserialization and subsequent object merging/assigning flows frequently trigger prototype pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4fc774259228cd414282425d0a281e37-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0afc271ddfef476dba107d5eebef758e-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-conf-06-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-22-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582916</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nested JSON Request Body">
  <data key="d0">Nested JSON Request Body</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A condition where input data is structured as nested objects in a JSON request body, making it susceptible to injection attacks within that hierarchy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raw JSON Operators Preserved">
  <data key="d0">Raw JSON Operators Preserved</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A condition where the NoSQL API does not strip or normalize reserved JSON operators before processing.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition where JSON documents submitted to an API retain dangerous operators (e.g., `$`, `{`, `}`) without proper sanitization for NoSQL query contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NoSQL Operator Injection Payload">
  <data key="d0">NoSQL Operator Injection Payload</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A reusable input string containing NoSQL operators intended to alter query logic, bypassing sanitization.&lt;SEP&gt;A reusable, parameterized malicious string containing NoSQL syntax operators (such as `$` queries or array/object references) that aims to inject into the application's database call.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NoSQL Query Logic Change">
  <data key="d0">NoSQL Query Logic Change</data>
  <data key="d1">ObservableSignal</data>
  <data key="d2">Evidence indicating that malicious input has been parsed and altered the intended query execution in the database response.&lt;SEP&gt;An observable behavior indicating that submitted input has altered the intended logic or operation of a NoSQL database query (e.g., changing a filter condition).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JSON Request Body">
  <data key="d0">JSON Request Body</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A target condition where input data arrives within a JSON formatted request body.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MongoDB $where Operator">
  <data key="d0">MongoDB $where Operator</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">An exploitation action that injects malicious operators into a MongoDB `$where` function to alter query logic within the application layer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application Layer Parsing">
  <data key="d0">Application Layer Parsing</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Condition where NoSQL query strings are constructed and concatenated within application source code instead of a database driver mechanism.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency Checks Absence">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency Checks Absence</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Environmental condition specific to MongoDB where lack of traditional relational constraints allows injection payloads to bypass stricter SQL-style validation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="$where">
  <data key="d0">$where</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A reserved MongoDB query operator used for filtering and calculating results via JavaScript; it is also a valid PHP variable name enabling potential injection vectors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unsanitized Input">
  <data key="d0">Unsanitized Input</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">An input value passed into the MongoDB query without proper validation, filtering, or parameterization that permits injection attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;A target condition where user-supplied data is not properly sanitized or encoded before being used in rendering.&lt;SEP&gt;Malicious data injected into query parameters or variables, leading to unintended database commands.&lt;SEP&gt;Application request input that has not been properly validated, filtered, or escaped before being used in an ORM call.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-507feea21a2b9071c451f23d5f3c743d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7a6169ed7611988c7689307fbb72ef84-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7a6169ed7611988c7689307fbb72ef84-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-clnt-03-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-7-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785581307</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="arbitrary JavaScript">
  <data key="d0">arbitrary JavaScript</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">Reusable malicious script code inserted into vulnerable operator fields to manipulate queries, trigger unauthorized actions, or cause DoS conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MongoDB query malformed error">
  <data key="d0">MongoDB query malformed error</data>
  <data key="d1">ObservableSignal</data>
  <data key="d2">A detectable response indicating that the database rejected the query due to syntax corruption or unexpected input that suggests unsafe handling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Denial of Service by CPU Spam">
  <data key="d0">Denial of Service by CPU Spam</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackGoal</data>
  <data key="d2">The attacker's objective to cause sustained high CPU usage by forcing the MongoDB instance to perform resource-intensive looping evaluations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JSON evaluation allowed in query fields">
  <data key="d0">JSON evaluation allowed in query fields</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A target condition where MongoDB API fields permit evaluation of untrusted JSON or JavaScript expressions rather than strictly using parameterized queries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parameterized Query">
  <data key="d0">Parameterized Query</data>
  <data key="d1">DefensiveControl</data>
  <data key="d2">A security practice where user input is passed as a placeholder parameter instead of being concatenated directly into query strings, preventing injection attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHP Variable Name Injection Vector">
  <data key="d0">PHP Variable Name Injection Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">Exploiting the fact that MongoDB query operators like `$where` are valid PHP variable names, allowing an attacker to submit input that gets interpreted as a PHP variable assignment instead of parameter value.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4bbd60ae8a8276a511522c70e38b3d51-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-6-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Username">
  <data key="d0">Username</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A parameter or field that represents the user's username as part of a login or authentication mechanism.&lt;SEP&gt;Target element within login credentials used to test valid account identification.&lt;SEP&gt;Input candidate used during the enumeration process (valid or invalid).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-92b67b99fc7587fc9af9d2208dfa4918-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-92b67b99fc7587fc9af9d2208dfa4918-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-idnt-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785579853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Password">
  <data key="d0">Password</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A parameter or field that represents the user's password as part of a login or authentication mechanism.&lt;SEP&gt;Target element within login credentials used to test authentication validity.&lt;SEP&gt;The credential component used during the brute force attempts to discover the correct password for a guessed username.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-92b67b99fc7587fc9af9d2208dfa4918-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-92b67b99fc7587fc9af9d2208dfa4918-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-idnt-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785579853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Database">
  <data key="d0">Database</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">The backend data storage system that is targeted and queried during SQL injection testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b515367113bce44cf3564abc5019508d-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SQL Query">
  <data key="d0">SQL Query</data>
  <data key="d1">Artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A structured string that instructs a relational database how to retrieve, insert, update or delete data.&lt;SEP&gt;An SQL statement constructed to test database logic and authentication mechanisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Comment Delimiter (e.g., -- or /* */)">
  <data key="d0">Comment Delimiter (e.g., -- or /* */)</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A malicious input pattern that uses comment syntax to ignore the rest of the query at the database.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LIMIT Operator">
  <data key="d0">LIMIT Operator</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">An SQL syntax piece used to restrict query results or force a specific number of records to be returned.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Authentication Logic">
  <data key="d0">Authentication Logic</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">A flaw in the application's verification process where database results determine authentication rather than verified credentials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="String Concatenation">
  <data key="d0">String Concatenation</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A method of appending malicious characters or values to input fields to modify query structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single Quote Escaping (Bypass)">
  <data key="d0">Single Quote Escaping (Bypass)</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">An action that closes SQL syntax quotes incorrectly to break the normal expression parsing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boolean Condition (Always True)">
  <data key="d0">Boolean Condition (Always True)</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">An attack payload containing a logical condition that always evaluates to TRUE, such as '1'='1'.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single Quote SQL Injection Probe">
  <data key="d0">Single Quote SQL Injection Probe</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific action inserting single quotes to manipulate SQL syntax for injection testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OR Logic Attack Pattern">
  <data key="d0">OR Logic Attack Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A parameterized input string using `OR` condition to force a boolean true evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blind SQL Injection">
  <data key="d0">Blind SQL Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">A variant of SQL Injection where the application does not show direct database feedback (e.g., errors or data in responses) to the attacker.&lt;SEP&gt;A target condition where application responses do not reveal data directly; only behavioral signals indicate successful exploitation.&lt;SEP&gt;A SQL injection weakness class where the server does not return error messages, pages, or distinguishable content to reveal input-handling behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;A database vulnerability where an attacker sends crafted input but cannot directly observe success based on response data.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of exploiting SQL injection using indirect means such as response timing or result sets to extract data without visible errors.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique where attacker injects SQL payloads without directly viewing the result data, relying on server responses (e.g., output differences or time changes) to infer information.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e673fd83bcb14789984668dec20fbae1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-240337c7993893650c60e2b94d64ba3c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-3-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-5-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785581287</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GET Request">
  <data key="d0">GET Request</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">GET Request is a client HTTP request method used to send data to a server for processing or retrieval. Explicitly noted for its role in information gathering, it transmits query string parameters in the URL and is commonly used to retrieve data from a server based on supplied URL parameters. While serving legitimate purposes like fetching web pages or submitting form data through parameters, the GET method is also significant from a security perspective. Since parameters are sent within the URL, they are often visible in network logs and can expose sensitive information, such as session identifiers, if not properly secured. Furthermore, due to its capability to send attacker-controlled input via URL parameters, an attacker can potentially leverage the GET method to transmit injection payloads, making it a target for security assessments and mitigations related to injection vulnerabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Request method used in example to perform delete operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4d9258074adf66922ee55cf68a3d1139-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4d9258074adf66922ee55cf68a3d1139-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3be59af692385f8910776899901b815f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3be59af692385f8910776899901b815f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1b71020ce840e0a943e1deda0b9f2c41-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2138db10de64fa713c14c45b2f2d6483-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35af4670be2579d771f8aacc9dfbff92-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-info-06-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-04-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-15-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-sess-04-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-sess-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785583538</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP Request Function (`UTL_HTTP.request` or similar)">
  <data key="d0">HTTP Request Function (`UTL_HTTP.request` or similar)</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">An Oracle DBMS function used to create out-of-band connections.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Out-Of-Band Channel">
  <data key="d0">Out-Of-Band Channel</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackGoal</data>
  <data key="d2">A strategic result where results of database queries are exfiltrated over a separate communication channel.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time Delay Signal">
  <data key="d0">Time Delay Signal</data>
  <data key="d1">ObservableSignal</data>
  <data key="d2">Server response time used as indirect evidence regarding the truth of an injected condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sleep Function Call">
  <data key="d0">Sleep Function Call</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A crafted SQL function call (e.g., `sleep()`) used to induce detectable delays.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dynamic SQL Execution">
  <data key="d0">Dynamic SQL Execution</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">To execute unauthorized queries within stored procedures or in-line SQL statements to retrieve sensitive data.&lt;SEP&gt;A target condition where SQL dynamically builds and executes queries based on user input.&lt;SEP&gt;An attack method where user input is injected into SQL queries that are dynamically constructed by the application.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unvalidated User Input">
  <data key="d0">Unvalidated User Input</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A condition where input fields accept data without proper validation or sanitization before use in back-end SQL queries.&lt;SEP&gt;Target condition where application input is not correctly sanitized or encoded before being incorporated into LDAP server requests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db3753bb1b28c5b9ebe5769dc9c06af2-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-06-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416495</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ReportColumnList">
  <data key="d0">ReportColumnList</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">The user-provided column list parameter in the dynamic reporting query that can be manipulated to influence the SQL.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Users Table">
  <data key="d0">Users Table</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">The target database table containing user credentials and other sensitive data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Users Table (password field)">
  <data key="d0">Users Table (password field)</data>
  <data key="d1">Artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A target data object whose values the attacker attempts to retrieve via unauthorized query.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SQL Injection Payload">
  <data key="d0">SQL Injection Payload</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A malicious input string injected into user-supplied fields to manipulate SQL statements; reusable variants include boolean-style (' or '1'='1'), union-based (' UNION SELECT ...'), comment-based suppression, and variant encodings like hexadecimal, URL-encoded, or case variation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ASP/VB Script Dynamic SQL">
  <data key="d0">ASP/VB Script Dynamic SQL</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique using VBScript string concatenation within SQL parameters to inject malicious queries despite validation layers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416486</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SQL Injection Vulnerability Class">
  <data key="d0">SQL Injection Vulnerability Class</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">A weakness class where user input is improperly concatenated into SQL queries, allowing unauthorized database interaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="User Input Fields (form fields)">
  <data key="d0">User Input Fields (form fields)</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Input fields in web forms (including hidden POST fields) that accept data later inserted into SQL queries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP Headers">
  <data key="d0">HTTP Headers</data>
  <data key="d1">Artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Additional transport-level inputs that can be tested for SQL injection.&lt;SEP&gt;A set of HTTP request headers that contain metadata such as authorization, cookies, content type, and server details.&lt;SEP&gt;Transport-layer fields such as `User-Agent`, `Referer`, or custom headers that may be used to inject SSI directives when dynamically generated content is built from header values.&lt;SEP&gt;Request and response metadata examined for Session ID transmission and security directives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4d9258074adf66922ee55cf68a3d1139-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8572f501a826eda55dfbb3563ca66b07-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2138db10de64fa713c14c45b2f2d6483-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-info-06-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-08-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-sess-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785583469</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP Cookies">
  <data key="d0">HTTP Cookies</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Session or application state values that may be included in SQL queries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SQL Query Construction Flow">
  <data key="d0">SQL Query Construction Flow</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">Application logic that builds SQL statements dynamically based on user input.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Database Management System (DBMS)">
  <data key="d0">Database Management System (DBMS)</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">Backend database software whose specific behavior influences SQL injection techniques.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Union-Based SQL Injection Technique">
  <data key="d0">Union-Based SQL Injection Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A method where two separate SQL queries joined by `UNION` are constructed to bypass filtering in one query while extracting data from another.&lt;SEP&gt;A SQL injection method that combines UNION to append attacker-controlled results to legitimate query output.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blind SQL Injection Technique">
  <data key="d0">Blind SQL Injection Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A method where database responses (e.g., page changes, error differences, response timing) are used instead of direct output.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Error-Based SQL Injection Technique">
  <data key="d0">Error-Based SQL Injection Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique that forces the database to generate error messages revealing data through disguised outputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boolean Logic Operators (AND/OR)">
  <data key="d0">Boolean Logic Operators (AND/OR)</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">Parameterized input patterns using logical operators for testing query logic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Valid User Credentials">
  <data key="d0">Valid User Credentials</data>
  <data key="d1">Artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Expected input values used as a baseline to differentiate normal application behavior from injection responses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Valid Intent Signal (e.g., product description)">
  <data key="d0">Valid Intent Signal (e.g., product description)</data>
  <data key="d1">ObservableSignal</data>
  <data key="d2">Legitimate application response indicating normal query execution and user authentication success.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Error Message">
  <data key="d0">Error Message</data>
  <data key="d1">ObservableSignal</data>
  <data key="d2">Application response containing database or application errors that reveal underlying database behavior or data.&lt;SEP&gt;Server-generated output indicating whether an input injection attempt succeeded or failed.&lt;SEP&gt;A response message or behavior indicating whether input was accepted or rejected by the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ab2f517f1c742be6f3d510cb76dc6940-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-09-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416528</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blank Page Response">
  <data key="d0">Blank Page Response</data>
  <data key="d1">ObservableSignal</data>
  <data key="d2">A lack of content or empty page returned when SQL query logic fails.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Database-Specific Pseudo-Functions (e.g., SUBSTRING)">
  <data key="d0">Database-Specific Pseudo-Functions (e.g., SUBSTRING)</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">Reusable function templates for extracting data character by character in blind SQL injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inband SQL Injection">
  <data key="d0">Inband SQL Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A variant where the attacker extracts database data through the same channel that injected the SQL code, typically displayed in the application response.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Out-of-band SQL Injection">
  <data key="d0">Out-of-band SQL Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A variant where data is extracted using a different channel (e.g., email, DNS, HTTP callback) instead of being returned in the application output.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inferential SQL Injection">
  <data key="d0">Inferential SQL Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A variant where no data is transferred to the attacker but database behavior changes based on crafted queries to infer information.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Union Operator Technique">
  <data key="d0">Union Operator Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">Appends a second valid SQL query to the original query using a UNION clause to merge and leak database contents.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boolean-Based SQL Injection">
  <data key="d0">Boolean-Based SQL Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A testing action that evaluates query logic by appending boolean expressions such as "AND 1=1" to determine SQL statement interpretation.&lt;SEP&gt;Uses boolean expressions (true/false conditions) to guess database content via page responses or error patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;A core SQL injection technique that uses true/false responses to infer data by injecting boolean conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-df80c9fe91c3765dc83d86ba6565a823-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-2-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785581177</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Error-Based SQL Injection">
  <data key="d0">Error-Based SQL Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">A testing action where the tester injects syntactically invalid SQL to provoke database error responses exposing structure or data.&lt;SEP&gt;Forces the database to generate distinctive error messages to deduce database structure and refine subsequent queries.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique that injects deliberate SQL syntax errors to trigger informative error messages from the database.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-240337c7993893650c60e2b94d64ba3c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;wstg-inpv-05-5-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785581287</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time Delay-based SQL Injection">
  <data key="d0">Time Delay-based SQL Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">Delays application responses using database commands such as `SLEEP()` to confirm query execution based on response timing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8f69c7e813a7c5f87c603a15e656cb8-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-05-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785416487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multiple SQL Queries Injection">
  <data key="d0">Multiple SQL Queries Injection</data>
