  <data key="d0">Directory Browsing</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">An action that attempts to access the contents of directories on a web server when directory listing is unintentionally enabled.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vulnerability Scanner">
  <data key="d0">Vulnerability Scanner</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">Automated software tool used to identify misconfigurations like directory browsing in a target infrastructure.&lt;SEP&gt;A tool used to scan web infrastructure to identify potential attack surfaces, discover hidden applications, or detect misconfigurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hidden Web Application">
  <data key="d0">Hidden Web Application</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A web application that is reachable via a specific URL but not linked from any published page or entry point.Description: A web application hosted on a network that can be accessed only if its exact URL is known, often resulting in undocumented or non-advertised services on the same domain or IP.&lt;SEP&gt;A web application whose presence is not explicitly advertised or discoverable through standard means, meaning explicit knowledge of its symbolic name or location is required for access.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard HTTP Port">
  <data key="d0">Standard HTTP Port</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Port 80 of TCP/IP used for unencrypted HTTP connections.Description: A common target port expectation where web applications are normally listening for incoming HTTP traffic.&lt;SEP&gt;The common port 80 used for HTTP connections and port 443 used for HTTPS connections, which are the default assumptions for web applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard HTTPS Port">
  <data key="d0">Standard HTTPS Port</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Port 443 of TCP/IP used for encrypted HTTPS connections.Description: A common target port expectation where web applications are normally listening for incoming HTTPS traffic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arbitrary TCP Port">
  <data key="d0">Arbitrary TCP Port</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Any non-standard port number beyond the standard 80 and 443 (e.g., ports outside 0–1023 or specific non-default ranges).Description: A target port condition representing web applications running on non-conventional TCP ports that defy simple port 80/443 scanning assumptions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DNS Name">
  <data key="d0">DNS Name</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A symbolic domain name (e.g., www.example.com) associated with one or more IP addresses and numerical ports for web access.Description: A hostname that can resolve to multiple web applications on a single IP via virtual hosting or 1-to-N mappings.&lt;SEP&gt;A symbolic name resolved to an IP address via DNS, such as `helpdesk.example.com` or `webmail.example.com`.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Host">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Host</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanism that allows multiple domain names or hostnames to map to a single IP address while being distinguished inside server software (e.g., Apache/NGINX config).Description: A server-side configuration enabling one IP to serve distinct web applications based on the requested Host header from the HTTP request.&lt;SEP&gt;A concept in web servers that allows a single IP address to serve different content based on DNS names or HTTP Host headers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hidden URL">
  <data key="d0">Hidden URL</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A web address not linked externally but pointing directly to an application endpoint on standard or non-standard ports.Description: A complete URL pattern such as `&lt;scheme&gt;://&lt;host&gt;/&lt;path&gt;` that is reachable but not published or linked from any standard web page.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Web Search Engine">
  <data key="d0">Web Search Engine</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A web-based tool that allows querying indexed web content using operators (e.g., site: operator) to discover hidden or non-advertised URLs.Description: A service such as Google, Bing, or DuckDuckGo that can be queried with domain-specific filters to find mentions of unknown subdomains or paths.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="site Operator">
  <data key="d0">site Operator</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">A search-engine-specific query modifier (e.g., site:www.example.com) used to restrict results to a single domain.Description: A restriction keyword in web search queries that limits results to pages within the specified registered domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Port Scanner Tool">
  <data key="d0">Port Scanner Tool</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A software utility that probes IP addresses across all TCP/UDP ports to detect open services.Description: An automated tool capable of scanning every port on an IP range to identify running services, commonly using options like `-sV` for version detection on open ports.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard Non-HTTP Port">
  <data key="d0">Standard Non-HTTP Port</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A TCP/IP port number in the 64k range other than the standard 80 or 443, where web applications might unexpectedly be listening.Description: A potential target port condition for discovering hidden web applications not bound to conventional HTTP or HTTPS defaults.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-info-04-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785580147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Explicit URL Knowledge">
  <data key="d0">Explicit URL Knowledge</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackerCapability</data>
  <data key="d2">Known exact URL path or address required to access applications not linked from published pages.Description: The attacker already possesses knowledge of the specific, hidden URL structure needed to reach the Target Application directly because they know it exists or have obtained it (e.g., `app1.example.com:8080/admin`).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-af827feaaf7735b8242b6a751ee616fa-chunk-002</data>
