</node>
<node id="HTTP Request Smuggling">
  <data key="d0">HTTP Request Smuggling</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">An attack technique that exploits inconsistencies in HTTP protocol parsing between different servers or intermediaries to bypass input validation constraints.&lt;SEP&gt;A class of vulnerabilities resulting from inconsistent request parsing between frontend and backend components, leading to hidden request injection or data leakage.&lt;SEP&gt;An attack methodology that exploits HTTP parsing inconsistencies between front-end and back-end servers by injecting second requests into the header/body buffer.&lt;SEP&gt;A class of vulnerabilities caused by inconsistencies in how HTTP requests are parsed between frontend components and backend servers. Unexpected header interpretations lead to request boundary desynchronization, enabling hidden requests to be processed out of sequence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582540</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
