  <data key="d0">HTTP Protocol Version Inconsistency&lt;|VulnerabilityClass|&gt;</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">A weakness class where protocol interpretation differs between client and server stacks, enabling header/body injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582540</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RFC-compliant Parsing">
  <data key="d0">RFC-compliant Parsing</data>
  <data key="d1">DefensiveControl</data>
  <data key="d2">Enforcement of correct HTTP protocol handling according to RFC standards to prevent request splitting and smuggling attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582540</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protocol Downgrades">
  <data key="d0">Protocol Downgrades</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">Inconsistent specification compliance allowing older or non-standard protocol behavior that can be misinterpreted across connection layers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582540</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intermediary Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Intermediary Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">Any proxy, load balancer, reverse proxy, or HTTP gateway device that processes incoming requests before forwarding them to the backend server.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582540</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backend Server">
  <data key="d0">Backend Server</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">Target application or web server that processes HTTP requests and contains input validation logic which can be bypassed via smuggling attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP/2 Protocol">
  <data key="d0">HTTP/2 Protocol</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A modern network protocol that can be downgraded to HTTP/1.1 by intermediaries, creating translation points where request parsing may diverge from backend expectations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP/1.1 Protocol">
  <data key="d0">HTTP/1.1 Protocol</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">A classic HTTP protocol whose request parsing rules are used by backend servers, often differing from how HTTP/2 sent data is normalized by intermediaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cleartext HTTP/2 (H2C)">
  <data key="d0">Cleartext HTTP/2 (H2C)</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanism where HTTP/2 frames are transported over HTTP/1.1 connections, creating protocol transition points that can introduce parsing mismatches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protocol Translation Logic">
  <data key="d0">Protocol Translation Logic</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">A weakness in intermediary code that reconstructs or modifies requests when downgrading HTTP/2 to HTTP/1.1 or upgrading cleartext, often leading to request boundary mismatches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Header Normalization">
  <data key="d0">Header Normalization</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Target behavior where intermediary systems rewrite or standardize HTTP headers before forwarding requests to backend servers, sometimes creating conflicts with backend parsing assumptions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Content-Length (CL) Header">
  <data key="d0">Content-Length (CL) Header</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">An HTTP header indicating message length; its presence and interpretation differ between protocol versions, historically causing desynchronization with Transfer-Encoding headers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer-Encoding (TE) Header">
  <data key="d0">Transfer-Encoding (TE) Header</data>
  <data key="d1">TechnologyStack</data>
  <data key="d2">An HTTP header specifying how message body should be encoded; inconsistencies between frontend/translator and backend processing of this header enable request smuggling attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Request Boundary Desynchronization">
  <data key="d0">Request Boundary Desynchronization</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackGoal</data>
  <data key="d2">A condition where frontend and backend components disagree on where a request begins or ends due to differing parser states or normalization policies.&lt;SEP&gt;Outcome where an attacker injects a second malformed request hidden in buffered data after an incorrect or partial request closing, allowing the second request to be processed before expected.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backend Server Parsing Assumptions">
  <data key="d0">Backend Server Parsing Assumptions</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
  <data key="d2">Target condition defining how the server interprets request termination based on RFC rules, which becomes exploitable when intermediaries modify requests differently.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Whitespace Manipulation">
  <data key="d0">Whitespace Manipulation</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">Concrete testing method where varying carriage returns, line feeds, or spaces are injected into requests to confuse parser state across different components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Header Duplication">
  <data key="d0">Header Duplication</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">Concrete testing method involving repeating HTTP headers with differing values to test how different components prioritize or interpret duplicate header fields causing parsing conflicts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-standard Separators">
  <data key="d0">Non-standard Separators</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d2">Concrete testing method that injects unexpected or malformed request delimiters to probe how frontend and backend parsers handle request closure differently.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second Request Bypass">
  <data key="d0">Second Request Bypass</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackGoal</data>
  <data key="d2">Attacker objective of having the backend process additional requests hidden in unacknowledged request trailers or leftover buffered data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8b6431100fb5694f9e64efa595cebe6c-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">wstg-inpv-16-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785582541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
